 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 12,
   "y": 17,
   "text": "WANLOCKHEAD. About 467 metres above the sea, the highest village in Scotland. Lead was dug out of these hills for centuries, and the old workings are kept as the Museum of Lead Mining. The miners paid a subscription to keep a library of their own, and the Miners' Library is still here. Books were the other thing they dug for.",
   "plaque": "w55_sc_wanlockhead",
   "title": "Wanlockhead"
  },
  {
   "x": 19,
   "y": 17,
   "text": "SOUTHERN UPLAND WAY. 212 miles, Portpatrick to Cockburnspath, opened in 1984 as Scotland's first coast-to-coast long-distance path. The waymarks are a thistle in a hexagon. You are on the bit between the lead hills and the Borders, and it is as good as the rest."
  },
  {
   "x": 43,
   "y": 22,
   "text": "TIBBIE SHIELS. A widow called Isabella Richardson, known as Tibbie Shiels, took in lodgers at this cottage on St Mary's Loch from the 1820s and went on doing it for the rest of a long life. Walter Scott and James Hogg are both said to have called, and so did a great many walkers who were glad of the fire. The inn still has her name.",
   "plaque": "w55_sc_tibbie",
   "title": "Tibbie Shiels"
  },
  {
   "x": 34,
   "y": 23,
   "text": "JAMES HOGG, THE ETTRICK SHEPHERD. Shepherd turned poet, who wrote The Private Memoirs and Confessions of a Justified Sinner in 1824. The statue looks across the loch he grew up beside, with a dog at his feet, because of course."
  }
 ],
